@meta
id: 31-12
title: Treesitter is a parser, not a colour scheme
chapter: 31
type: lesson
skills: treesitter, lua-config
par: 72
minutes: 7
vscode: semantic highlighting, which you never had to switch on
open: treesitter.lua

@brief
Vim highlights code with regular expressions. A syntax file is a few
hundred patterns saying "this looks like a string, that looks like a
keyword", and it is wrong about as often as a regex is wrong about a
programming language — template literals, JSX, nested quotes, generics.

Treesitter replaces the guess with a parse. Neovim embeds the tree-sitter
library, so opening a TypeScript file builds an actual syntax tree of that
file and keeps it up to date as you type, incrementally, per keystroke.
Highlighting is then a query over the tree: not "a word after `class`"
but "the name node of a class declaration".

What the tree buys you, beyond better colours:

- **Structural text objects.** With `nvim-treesitter-textobjects`, `vaf`
  selects a function and `vac` a class — the same grammar as `vap`, over
  code structure instead of blank lines. This is the one that changes how
  you edit.
- **Folds and indent** derived from the tree rather than from `foldmethod=indent`.
- **`:InspectTree`**, which shows you the parse of the buffer you are
  looking at, and **`:Inspect`**, which tells you what highlight group is
  under the cursor and why.

Three honest limits. Treesitter has nothing to do with LSP — one parses
the file in front of you, the other asks a compiler about the whole
project, and you want both for different reasons. Parsers are compiled C
libraries that have to be installed per language and kept in step with the
plugin. And when a parser and its queries disagree after an update, your
highlighting breaks in a way a regex syntax file never does.

The config in front of you installs two parsers and has indentation turned
off. Add `json` and `markdown` to the list, after `typescript`, and switch
tree-sitter indentation on.

Graded in vim. The Lua is written, not run — there is no parser to install
here.

@keys
| `f'` `a` | to the closing quote, then append after it |
| `ciw` | change the word under the cursor |

@hints
Two edits: one inside the list, one boolean.
--
The list is `{ 'lua', 'typescript' }`. Append to it rather than retyping
it: get to the quote that closes `'typescript'` and carry on from there.
--
`/typescript` Enter, `f'`, `a`, then `, 'json', 'markdown'`. Then
`/false` Enter and `ciwtrue`.

@solution
    /typescript<Enter> f' a, 'json', 'markdown'<Esc>
    /false<Enter> ciw true <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Under sixty keystrokes, and both edits are ones you have made a hundred
times in this dojo on TypeScript. Config files are just files.

A note on where this is going. `nvim-treesitter` is the plugin that
installs parsers and wires up the modules, but the tree-sitter integration
itself is core Neovim — `vim.treesitter.start()` in an ftplugin is enough
to get highlighting for a parser you already have. Over time more of this
plugin's job is moving into the editor, which is the same arc `vim.lsp`
followed: plugin first, core later.

And the thing to hold on to when the ecosystem argues about it: treesitter
gives you *better information about the file*. It does not give you a new
way to move around it. `}`, `%`, `di{` and `f(` still do what they always
did, and they work on the server you ssh into tomorrow, which has none of
this.

@start treesitter.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/lua/config/treesitter.lua

require('nvim-treesitter.configs').setup({
  ensure_installed = { 'lua', 'typescript' },
  highlight = { enable = true },
  indent = { enable = false },
})

@expected treesitter.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/lua/config/treesitter.lua

require('nvim-treesitter.configs').setup({
  ensure_installed = { 'lua', 'typescript', 'json', 'markdown' },
  highlight = { enable = true },
  indent = { enable = true },
})
